shards.content:
`std::unique_ptr<T>` owns an object and invokes its deleter when needed. It can be moved but cannot be copied.

```cpp
#include <memory>
#include <utility>

auto first = std::make_unique<int>(7);
auto second = std::move(first);
// second owns the int and first is null
```

Caveat: `get()` supplies a borrowed pointer. It neither creates another owner nor keeps the pointee alive.

`std::move(x)` casts an expression to an rvalue reference. The cast itself transfers nothing. A constructor or assignment selected afterward may implement a move.

```cpp
#include <string>
#include <utility>

std::string original = "hello";
auto&& alias = std::move(original);
// original is still "hello"
std::string destination = std::move(alias);
```

Caveat: a moved-from standard-library object is generally valid with an unspecified value unless its type promises more. Do not assume a moved-from string is empty. See the [moved-from rule](https://eel.is/c++draft/lib.types.movedfrom).

`const T&` gives read-only access through that reference. It does not make a non-const object immutable through every other alias.

```cpp
int value = 3;
const int& view = value;
value = 4;
// view now observes 4
// view = 5 would be ill-formed
```

Caveat: borrowing through a reference does not generally prolong an existing object's lifetime. A reference can outlive the object it names.

When a vector reallocates, its old element pointers, references, and iterators are invalidated. Reserve capacity before taking borrowed element access if you need to avoid later growth reallocations.

```cpp
#include <vector>

std::vector<int> values{1};
const int& first = values[0];
values.reserve(values.capacity() + 1);
// first is now dangling and must not be read
```

Caveat: reserve does not increase size. It also does not prevent invalidation caused by insertion or erase at affected positions. See the [modifier rules](https://eel.is/c++draft/vector.modifiers).

For undefined behavior, the C++ standard imposes no requirements on the resulting behavior. A crash is not required, and a successful run does not establish correctness.

```cpp
#include <limits>

int value = std::numeric_limits<int>::max();
// ++value would overflow signed int
// Do not execute that increment
```

Caveat: compilation errors and undefined behavior are different categories. Some invalid programs require a diagnostic. Signed arithmetic overflow is covered by the [expression rule](https://eel.is/c++draft/expr.pre).

An in-class member function body is a complete-class context. Its member lookup can see a nested type declared later in that class.

```cpp
class Example {
public:
    int read() const {
        Later value{7};
        return value.number;
    }
private:
    struct Later { int number; };
};
```

Caveat: this does not permit arbitrary use before declaration. Writing `Later read()` before declaring `Later` does not get the same rule for that return type. Templates add separate lookup considerations. See [complete-class contexts](https://eel.is/c++draft/class.mem.general).

Misconception: once a reference names an object, the object must remain alive for as long as the reference exists.

A reference is borrowed access, not an owner. Resetting the owner can destroy the object while the reference remains.

```cpp
#include <memory>

auto owner = std::make_unique<int>(7);
const int& borrowed = *owner;
owner.reset();
// borrowed is dangling and must not be read
```

Caveat: some direct bindings extend a temporary's lifetime under specific rules. That does not apply to this already-owned object.
